            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "p12-q12-task-paragraph",
          "type": "paragraph",
          "fontSize": null,
          "align": "left",
          "spaceBefore": 0,
          "spaceAfter": 1,
          "lineHeight": 1.4,
          "indent": 0,
          "inlines": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "text": "Prove that the product of the two oblong numbers formed by multiplying the first two and the last two of three consecutive integers is an oblong number.",
              "marks": []
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "The product is $(n^2-1)n^2$, an oblong number.\n\nUse the difference of two squares; $n^2-1$ and $n^2$ are consecutive integers.",
      "worked": "Let the three consecutive integers be $n-1$, $n$ and $n+1$. The two oblong numbers are $(n-1)n$ and $n(n+1)$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}[(n-1)n][n(n+1)]&=(n-1)(n+1)n^2\\\\{}[(n-1)n][n(n+1)]&=(n^2-1)n^2.\\end{align*}$$\n\nSince $n^2-1$ and $n^2$ are consecutive integers, their product is an oblong number.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 45
  },
